{"authored" [{"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nInterpret the printed inequalities literally. If they define a degenerate set, use the zero-area surface-integral convention. What is the flux?\n\nHint: First check whether $-x\\le y\\le0$ can have a nonempty interval when $x\\le0$. Treat the printed inequalities literally." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The original inequalities are retained literally. Since $-\\pi\\le x\\le0$, we have $-x\\ge0$. The additional inequalities $-x\\le y\\le0$ are possible only when $-x=0$, so $x=0$ and $y=0$. Therefore the projection is $D=\\{(0,0)\\}$ and its two-dimensional area is zero. The graph $z=\\cos(x+y)$ over $D$ consists only of $(0,0,1)$; it is a degenerate surface, not a regular two-dimensional patch.\n\nUnder the zero-area surface-integral convention, both its surface area and its flux are zero:\n\n$$\\operatorname{area}(S)=0,\\qquad\\iint_S\\langle1,0,y\\rangle\\cdot d\\mathbf S=0.$$\n\nOne may extend the upward graph vector area element from the surrounding smooth graph: $(-g_x,-g_y,1)=(\\sin(x+y),\\sin(x+y),1)$. Its dot product with $F=\\langle1,0,y\\rangle$ is $\\sin(x+y)+y$. Integrating this bounded continuous function over the literal zero-area set $D$ yields zero.\n\nThe source accepted choice b, $\\pi-\\pi^3/6$, and its revealed solution instead evaluated the formal signed repeated integral\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\int_{-x}^{0}(\\sin(x+y)+y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\left(1-\\cos x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\,dx=\\pi-\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}.$$\n\nFor $x<0$, these inner limits are reversed. A formal signed integral over reversed limits does not integrate over the region described by the original inequalities. No corrected bounds are inferred. The study answer is $0$ for the original literal inequalities; the source's choice, grading and calculation remain preserved as evidence." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7u+4v"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7u-8v"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4u+7v"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "7u+8v"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8u-7v"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7u+4v"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)v\\cdot (-1)+(u-v)u\\cdot (1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-uv+{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}-uv+uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-w