rs/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x-y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-\\frac{15}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-\\frac{19}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle z+2y, \\: 3y+z, \\: x -z\\right\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-3x+2y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/assets/3761b12939f74f1e7f4d01ca83ea2884c1296672e22128064b109f7c42bb4df6.png)\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12383.json" "file_sha256" "0432e61837fed8f1206db122739b63de1d7d54702c47ae6d78559c37f9032551" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=-3u+2v} \\\\  & =⟨(-3u+2v)+2v,\\,3v+(-3u+2v),\\,u-(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-3u+4v)\\cdot (3)+(-3u+5v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+12v+6u-10v+4u-2v \\\\  & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}u\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12383.json" "file_sha256" "0432e61837fed8f1206db122739b63de1d7d54702c47ae6d78559c37f9032551" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \n\\Bigg( \n\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} \n+ Q \n- \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z}\n\\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf j$-component.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12387.json" "file_sha256" "023ff0544af3dcfde73910172096bbf14e10e2ae5d1542218868c21f068fad20" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12387.json" "file_sha256" "023ff0544af3dcfde73910172096bbf14e10e2ae5d1542218868c21f068fad20" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x, y, z) = \\left\\langle -y, \\: x, \\: z+2 \\right\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12357.json" "file_sha256" "9e103f5789edec7b5530d4f9dbf9ae301a72b05d12e7724a7f14de320599e9dc" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12357.json" "file_sha256" "9e103f5789edec7b5530d4f9dbf9ae301a72b05d12e7724a7f14de320599e9dc" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"e-12357" [] "e-12383" [] "q-161456" [] "q-161542" [] "q-161357" [] "q-161476" [] "e-12387" [] "q-161382" [] "q-161358" [] "q-161189" [] "q-161463" [] "q-161455" [] "q-161462" [] "q-161441" [] "q-161453" [] "q-161359" [] "q-161481" [] "q-161539" []} "basis" 2266 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186194094 17592186194095 17592186194096 17592186194097 17592186194098 17592186355779 17592186355780 17592186355781 17592186355782 17592186355783 17592186355786 17592186355787 17592186355788 17592186355789 17592186355790 17592186355793 17592186355794 17592186355795 17592186355796 17592186355797 17592186355800 17592186355801 17592186355802 17592186355803 17